From e39b43ccbad65610de9aac3c1736e3cd8e2ef860 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 20:42:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][Capture] The frame of the video alone at its own size

Issue #30. Kodi 22 captures the video apart from the menus (the video
tap of CRenderManager, TakeScreenshot(video)), but at the size the video
is drawn on the output: a 1080p film on a 4K output is saved upscaled,
and a video shown in a window is saved small. To compare frames (two
settings, two players) or to attach one to a report, the frame of the
film is wanted as it is, one pixel of the picture to one pixel of the
capture.

A capture of the video can now ask for that (CaptureSpec::videoOwnSize).
Its own size is that of the part of the picture that Kodi shows (the
source rectangle: the whole picture in the usual view, one eye of a 3D
video). It is served by the video tap, right after the picture is
presented and before subtitles, the OSD or the GUI are drawn over it
(CRenderManager::ServiceVideoFrame()), by the first of these that can
give it (VideoFrameCapture.h):

- Kodi's own renderer drew the picture whole in this frame
  (CLinuxRendererGLES with its GLSL shaders, not a field, not rotated):
  drawn in two passes, the picture of the first pass, which is at its
  own size before the scaler; drawn in one pass, the same picture drawn
  again with the same shader into a picture of its own size
  (CaptureOwnSize.cpp), without the display's 3D LUT. This is also the
  way where the video is shown larger, smaller, zoomed or stretched.
- The picture is drawn at its own size, at a whole pixel, all of it on
  the screen: it is read where it is drawn, as it is (the pictures that
  the Dolby Vision bridge draws, and the picture of native Dolby Vision
  output before it is packed for the link, never the transport).
- A way that draws only into the output drew it at another size, in its
  own proportions (the bridge scales in the pass that maps the picture,
  and a second draw would change what it measures of the picture on the
  screen): it is read at the size it is shown, and the result says so.
- Otherwise (zoomed past the edges of the screen, stretched) nothing is
  read, and the result says why.
A renderer that shows the video on a plane of its own draws its decoded
picture at its own size, as Kodi's video capture of it does
(CaptureVideoFrame()).

The capture is tagged as the output is coded, with the HDR metadata of
its picture, as a screenshot is. How it was made, and how many
milliseconds it held the thread that draws, go with it for kodi.log.

Nothing changes for a frame that no such capture waits for: the
renderer only notes which picture it drew whole with its own shaders.

Tests: TestVideoFrameCapture (where the frame comes from: drawn at its
own size, letterboxed, an eye of frame packing, the renderer whenever
it can, scaled, half a pixel off, zoomed, stretched, no picture).
